questions.content:
My useEffect cleanup doesn't seem to run when the component unmounts.

## Code

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const subscription = api.subscribe(data => {
    setData(data);
  });

  return () => {
    console.log("Cleanup running");
    subscription.unsubscribe();
  };
}, []);
```

## Problem

When I navigate away from the page, the cleanup log never appears and I get memory leak warnings.

{answer_react_cleanup}

answers.content:
This is a common issue! There are a few things to check:

## 1. Strict Mode Double-Mount

In React 18 with StrictMode, components mount, unmount, then mount again in development. This is intentional to help find bugs.

```jsx
// In your index.js
<React.StrictMode>
  <App />
</React.StrictMode>
```

The cleanup DOES run - it's just followed immediately by another mount.

## 2. Check Your Subscription API

Make sure `api.subscribe()` actually returns an object with `unsubscribe`:

```jsx
console.log(subscription); // Should have unsubscribe method
```

## 3. Use AbortController for Fetch

If you're fetching data, use AbortController:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();

  fetch('/api/data', { signal: controller.signal })
    .then(res => res.json())
    .then(setData)
    .catch(err => {
      if (err.name !== 'AbortError') throw err;
    });

  return () => controller.abort();
}, []);
```

## 4. Verify Component Actually Unmounts

Sometimes the component isn't unmounting - it's just being hidden:

```jsx
// This doesn't unmount:
{show && <Component />}

// Check if your router is caching pages
```

questions.content:
I'm trying to create a generic function but TypeScript isn't inferring types correctly.

## Code

```typescript
function createStore<T>(initial: T) {
  let state = initial;

  return {
    get: () => state,
    set: (value: T) => { state = value; }
  };
}

const store = createStore({ name: '', age: 0 });
store.set({ name: 'John' }); // Error: missing 'age'
```

## Expected

I want `set` to accept partial updates.

{answer_typescript_generic}

answers.content:
The issue is that `set` requires the full type. Here's how to fix it:

## Solution 1: Partial Updates

```typescript
function createStore<T extends object>(initial: T) {
  let state = initial;

  return {
    get: () => state,
    set: (value: Partial<T>) => {
      state = { ...state, ...value };
    }
  };
}

const store = createStore({ name: '', age: 0 });
store.set({ name: 'John' }); // Works!
```

## Solution 2: Separate Full/Partial Methods

```typescript
function createStore<T extends object>(initial: T) {
  let state = initial;

  return {
    get: () => state,
    set: (value: T) => { state = value; },
    update: (value: Partial<T>) => {
      state = { ...state, ...value };
    }
  };
}
```

## Solution 3: Use Immer for Complex State

```typescript
import produce from 'immer';

function createStore<T>(initial: T) {
  let state = initial;

  return {
    get: () => state,
    update: (recipe: (draft: T) => void) => {
      state = produce(state, recipe);
    }
  };
}

store.update(draft => {
  draft.name = 'John';
});
```

## Key TypeScript Tip

When you want partial updates, always use `Partial<T>` and spread to merge:

```typescript
state = { ...state, ...partialUpdate };
```
